  <ferc:RespondentLegalName id="f-36" contextRef="c-1">GridLiance Heartland LLC</ferc:RespondentLegalName>
  <ferc:ReportDate id="f-37" contextRef="c-1">2026-05-20</ferc:ReportDate>
  <ferc:RespondentLegalName id="f-38" contextRef="c-1">GridLiance Heartland LLC</ferc:RespondentLegalName>
  <ferc:ReportDate id="f-39" contextRef="c-1">2026-05-20</ferc:ReportDate>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-40" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;GRIDLIANCE HEARTLAND LLC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTES TO CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;FOR THE THREE MONTHS ENDED MARCH 31, 2026 and 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;(Dollars in thousands, unless otherwise noted)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting that varies from generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America ("GAAP"). These requirements differ from GAAP related to (1) the presentation of regulatory debits and credits (2) the presentation of deferred income taxes (3) the presentation of regulatory assets and liabilities (4) presentation of asset removal costs (5) the presentation of equity carrying charges. Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein. The information below is presented in millions unless otherwise stated. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements should be read in conjunction with the 2025 FERC Form 1 of GridLiance Heartland LLC. In the opinion of management, all adjustments considered necessary for fair financial statement presentation have been made. The results of operations for an interim period generally will not give a true indication of results for the year.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Note 1. Description of the Entity and Organization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;GridLiance Heartland LLC (&#8220;the Company&#8221;) was formed in 2014, is an indirect wholly-owned subsidiary of GridLiance Holdco LLC. (&#8220;GL&#8221;, &#8220;GridLiance LP&#8221;),&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; a Delaware limited liability company, which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Transmission, LLC ("NEET"). NEET is an indirect subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc. ("NextEra Energy"), a company listed on the New York Stock Exchange. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company, a single member limited liability company organized under the laws of Delaware, is a rate regulated transmission provider in Illinois and Kentucky. Its purpose is to collaborate with rural electric cooperatives, municipal utilities, joint action agencies and others to plan for the future of the grid within Mid-continent Independent System Operator, Inc. (&#8220;MISO&#8221;) located in the United States. The Company invests in necessary electric infrastructure and implements strategies to improve system reliability and resiliency and reduce overall costs to customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Note 2. Regulatory Matters &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Formula Rate Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company principally generates revenue through transmitting electricity to its customers through MISO. Substantially all of the Company&#8217;s material revenue streams consist of Commission-approved, tariff-based revenue. The projected revenue requirement for the transmission of electricity by the Company is calculated prior to the beginning of each year (the &#8220;Rate Year&#8221;) using the FERC-approved formula rate template and billed to and collected from the customers ratably over the Rate Year. The Company recognizes revenue proportionally throughout the year based on these approved cost-based formula rates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 29, 2019, the FERC accepted, subject to condition, the Company&#8217;s Formula Rate to determine and recover the costs of the Company&#8217;s investment in transmission facilities located in the MISO region. The Commission also approved the Company&#8217;s rate of return on equity ("ROE") of 10.82% including a 50-basis points ROE adder in recognition of the Company joining MISO, a regional transmission organization ("RTO") as transmission owner ("RTO Participation Adder"), subject to the outcome of pending proceedings. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 31, 2020, the Commission approved the implementation of the Company&#8217;s annual transmission revenue requirement ("ATRR") under its Formula Rate, effective upon placing the Company&#8217;s assets under MISO&#8217;s control, which occurred on March 1, 2020. The ATRR reflects an ROE of 10.38% (9.88% with a 50 basis point adder) and the Commission also approved tariff changes to implement the Rate Mitigation Credits proposed by the Company. The Rate Mitigation Credits provide for a fixed credit of $2.65 million on an annualized basis to offset the rate impact of including the first four lines in the MISO tariff, with such credit to be given for five years from closing of the Proposed Transaction (the Rate Mitigation Period). The Company&#8217;s Formula Rate also credits an additional fixed amount of $0.95 million to offset the rate impact of the remaining two lines for five years following the date they were added to the MISO tariff on August 31, 2022.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On February 21, 2020, to address the conditions on its Formula Rates imposed by the Commission&#8217;s order approving the acquisition of the EEI assets, the Company submitted a compliance filing providing for a continuation of the Rate Mitigation Credits for the two lines not in MISO for five years from the time they are added to the MISO tariff and providing that the Company may not recover its Regulatory Asset through rates during the initial five years of the Rate Mitigation Period.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On May 21, 2020, the Commission issued an order on rehearing refining its methodology and announcing a new policy for analyzing the base ROE component of public utility rates, which changed the MISO transmission owners' base ROE from 9.88% to 10.02%, which raised the Company&#8217;s ROE to 10.52%, inclusive of a 50-basis point ROE adder. In 2022, FERC's orders were vacated by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia and remanded back to the Commission for further consideration. On October 17, 2024, the Commission issued an &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Order on Remand whereby the MISO transmission owners' base ROE was set to 9.98%, resulting in a 10.48% ROE for the Company, inclusive of a 50-basis point ROE adder.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;For the two transmission lines that were not in the MISO region, on January 29, 2019, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s Open Access Transmission Tariff ("OATT") and Formula Rate, subject to conditions, refund, hearing and settlement judge procedures to establish its ROE. Following settlement discussions with the parties, on May 28, 2020, the Company filed an Offer of Settlement for a 9.8% ROE, which was approved via Commission Letter Order on September 28, 2020.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On September 30, 2020, MISO submitted a filing and revised tariff sheets in Docket No. ER20-3034 on behalf of the Company in compliance with Order No. 864, Public Utility Transmission Rate Changes to Address Accumulated Deferred Income Taxes. On September 28, 2020 in Docket No. ER20-3009, the Company made the same Order No. 864 compliance filing for the two transmission lines that were not in MISO. The Company submitted supplemental compliance filings in both dockets (filings in ER20-3034 submitted on May 14, 2025 and June 16, 2025; filing in ER20-3009 submitted June 24, 2025). Both sets of filings are&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; still pending Commission approval. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On April 1, 2021, the Company submitted tariff revisions to incorporate a new income tax allowance calculation for its assets that are not in MISO, consistent with a change in the Company&#8217;s upstream ownership. On March 31, 2021, the Company became a wholly owned, indirect subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc., a C corporation. Also on April 1, 2021, MISO submitted similar revisions on the Company&#8217;s behalf for the income tax allowance component of its revenue requirement for assets under MISO&#8217;s functional control. The Commission accepted both filings on May 20, 2021 via Letter Orders.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In November 2024, the Company agreed to acquire transmission assets from Logansport Utilities ("Logansport") for $5.0 million, contingent on receiving various state and federal regulatory approvals. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Filings to integrate the Logansport transmission assets into MISO were accepted by FERC on January 29, 2026 in Docket No. ER25-2766. The acquisition of Logansport was completed in February 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In 2025, the Company entered into a Joint Development Agreement with Dairyland Power Cooperative to jointly develop, construct, and own a portion of the North Rochester &#8211; Columbia 765 kV transmission line that was approved as part of MISO's LRTP Tranche 2.1 portfolio. On May 30, 2025 as amended on June 6, 2025, the Company filed in FERC Docket No. ER25-2391 a request for CWIP and Abandoned Plant incentives associated with the project. The Commission accepted the application on August 5, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Start-Up Regulatory Asset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 29, 2019 the FERC approved a transmission rate incentive pursuant to Section 205 of the Federal Power Act (&#8220;FPA&#8221;) that authorizes the Company to recover its prudently incurred, non-capitalized start-up costs through a Start-Up Regulatory Asset, effective on October 29, 2018. As discussed in the "Formula Rate Filings" section above, recovery of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;GLH&#8217;s Start-Up Regulatory &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Asset has been deferred.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; The Company will seek Commission approval of the total estimated amount of such costs incurred in the regulatory asset and the period of recovery through a subsequent FPA Section 205 filing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;State Regulatory Proceedings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 9, 2020, the Company filed a petition for declaratory ruling with the Illinois Commerce Commission ("ICC") seeking confirmation that the Company is not a public utility under Illinois law. If the ICC determines that the Company is a public utility, the Company requested that the Commission identify the applicability of certain statutes governing the standard of review that would apply in seeking a Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity ("CPCN") to operate in the state. On March 26, 2020, the ICC Administrative Law Judge ("ALJ") requested additional briefing on whether a declaratory ruling is the appropriate proceeding for determining whether the Company is a public utility. On June 6, 2020, the ICC issued an order denying the Company&#8217;s petition for declaratory ruling, stating that it would be more expedient to make that determination in the Ameren Illinois Company ("Ameren") complaint proceeding discussed below. On July 15, 2020, the Commission denied the Company&#8217;s request for rehearing. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 9, 2020, Ameren filed a complaint against the Company at the ICC, requesting that the ICC either find that the Company is in violation of the Illinois Public Utilities Act and, thus not lawfully operating as a public utility in Illinois or investigate whether the Company can operate as a public utility. A hearing was held in March 2022. On September 26, 2022, the ALJ issued a proposed interim order finding that the ICC does have subject matter jurisdiction and that GridLiance Heartland is a public utility and required to file a CPCN application. The ICC voted to approve the ALJ&#8217;s proposed interim order on November 17, 2022, requiring the Company to file a CPCN application by January 17, 2023. On December 27, 2023, the ALJ ruled that the Company was a public utility and the ICC was just in their order to require a CPCN. The Company appealed this interim order to the Illinois Appellate Court for the Fifth District, which appeal was denied on December 26, 2023. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 17, 2023, the Company filed its CPCN application. Ameren has filed leave to intervene and was granted party status. On October 12, 2023, the Commission ruled that the Company&#8217;s CPCN should include information regarding whether its ownership of the facilities is the least-cost means of serving the need for the facilities. The ALJ directed the Company to supplement its CPCN application by March 1, 2024, which supplement was timely filed. Ameren and ICC Staff filed testimony on June 17, 2024, the Company filed rebuttal testimony on July 22, 2024, and Ameren and ICC Staff filed rebuttal testimony on August 26, 2024. An evidentiary hearing was scheduled for&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; October 29-31, 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;. In late October, the parties notified the ALJ that they were engaged in settlement discussions and requested abatement of the hearing to allow the settlement to be finalized. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The parties filed the settlement agreement on December 11, 2024. Under the terms of the settlement, Ameren agreed not to oppose the Company's CPCN and to withdraw its pending complaint, subject to certain terms related to the Company's operations and maintenance of the assets and future infrastructure development. The ICC approved the settlement on &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;January 16, 2025, issuing the Company a CPCN. Per the terms of the settlement, Ameren filed with withdrew its complaint against the Company on February 24, 2025, which the ICC granted on March 13, 2025, resolving the complaint proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;NOTE 3 -UTILITY PLANT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Electric utility plant consists of the following:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(In Thousands)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:53.513%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.395%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.775%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:21.817%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr style="height:3pt"&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="6" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;March 31, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;December 31, 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Computer Software&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1,096&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1,088&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Land and Land Rights&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;574&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;574&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Structures and Improvements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;8,355&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;8,355&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Station Equipment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;19,696&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;19,686&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Towers and Fixtures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2,055&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2,055&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Poles and Fixtures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;6,789&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;6,789&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Overhead Conductors and Devices&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;5,327&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="backgroun